
Recursion and iteration are two ways of repeating work. Iteration uses a loop: you keep some state (a counter, an accumulator) and update it step by step until a condition says stop. Recursion has a function call itself on a smaller version of the same problem, trusting that the smaller call will return a correct answer, and then builds the full answer from that result.

What recursion gives you is a way of thinking. Many problems are naturally self-similar: a folder contains files and other folders, a tree node has subtrees, the factorial of n is n times the factorial of n−1. Recursion lets you write the code the way you'd describe the problem in words: "to solve this, solve the smaller version, then do one extra step." Every recursive function has two parts. The base case is the smallest version of the problem, which you answer directly without recursing. The recursive case reduces the problem and calls itself, making progress toward the base case.

def factorial(n):
    if n == 0:              # base case
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)   # recursive case: smaller problem

The cost is the call stack. Each function call gets a stack frame, a block of memory holding its local variables and where to return to. A frame stays on the stack until its call finishes. With recursion, the calls pile up: factorial(5) waits on factorial(4), which waits on factorial(3), and so on down to the base case. Only then do they unwind, each one finishing its multiplication. So recursion uses memory proportional to its depth, and each call also carries a small overhead of time. A loop doing the same job reuses one frame and constant memory. Any recursive function can be rewritten iteratively, sometimes by managing your own explicit stack. Recursion is usually chosen for clarity, especially on tree-shaped or divide-and-conquer problems, and iteration for efficiency on simple linear repetition.

Without a base case, or if the recursive step never actually moves toward it, the function keeps calling itself forever in principle. In practice every call pushes a new frame onto the call stack, and the stack has a limited size. Eventually it fills up and the program crashes with a stack overflow. In Python this appears as RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded, because Python caps recursion depth at about 1000 by default to fail safely instead of crashing the interpreter. In languages like C, the result can be a segmentation fault.

The "never reached" case is the more common bug, because a base case exists but the input slips past it. For example, if you call factorial(-1) using the code above, n goes −1, −2, −3… and never equals 0. A mistake in the recursive step, such as calling factorial(n) instead of factorial(n - 1), has the same effect. The takeaway is that a correct recursive function needs a base case, and every recursive call also needs to make the problem strictly smaller in a way that is guaranteed to reach that base case for every valid input. Contrast this with an infinite loop, which usually just hangs and uses no extra memory. Infinite recursion always ends in a crash, because memory runs out.

Factorial is a simple idea. The factorial of 5 means multiplying 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1, which equals 120. The factorial of 3 is 3 × 2 × 1, which is 6. You start at a number and multiply by every whole number below it, down to 1.

Look at the factorial of 5 again: 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1. Everything after the first 5 is 4 × 3 × 2 × 1, which is just the factorial of 4. So the factorial of 5 equals 5 times the factorial of 4. In the same way, the factorial of 4 equals 4 times the factorial of 3, and so on. The big problem contains a smaller copy of itself. Recursion is a method that uses this: to solve the big problem, you solve the slightly smaller one and add one extra step.

Picture a line of five people. You ask the first person, "What's the factorial of 5?" They don't want to do all the work, so they turn to the next person and ask, "What's the factorial of 4? When you tell me, I'll multiply it by 5." That person asks the next one about 3, who asks about 2, who asks about 1. Each person follows the same rule: pass a smaller question down the line and wait.

The last person is the stopping point. When asked for the factorial of 1, they don't ask anyone else. They just say "1," because that answer is simple enough to know on sight. Then the answers travel back up the line. The person holding 2 hears "1," multiplies, and says "2." The next hears 2, multiplies by 3, and says "6." Then 6 × 4 becomes 24, and finally the first person multiplies 24 by 5 and gives you 120.

The stopping point is essential. If nobody in the line were allowed to simply answer, every person would keep asking the next one forever, and nobody could reply, because each is waiting on someone else. With a computer, the line of waiting people keeps growing until the machine runs out of room to keep track of them, and the program fails. So every recursive process needs two things: a rule that makes the question a little smaller each time, and a question small enough to answer directly, so the chain eventually stops and the answers can come back.